{
  "title": "Add localhost bookmarklet capture for bot-walled pipeline URLs",
  "description": "Bucket B from the 2026-04-22 Paprika export is not a schema \
problem. These URLs never reach `extract_recipe` because fetch hits a cached \
403/429 from anti-bot or rate-limit middleware. Observed examples include Just \
One Cookbook, Serious Eats, The Spruce Eats, and Food52.\n\
\n\
The least-bad path here is a user-driven local capture flow instead of playing \
a permanent bot-evasion game in `reqwest`. Add a command that starts a small \
server on `localhost`, prints a bookmarklet, and accepts browser-posted page \
data for the current article. The intended workflow is:\n\
\n\
  1. run the command locally\n\
  2. open a naughty article in a real browser session that already passes the  \
    bot wall\n\
  3. invoke the bookmarklet\n\
  4. send the relevant page data back to the localhost server\n\
  5. save that captured payload into the local pipeline cache so later runs    \
  can extract and snapshot it without re-fetching\n\
\n\
Notes:\n\
\n\
  - this is explicitly allowed to be per-machine setup\n\
  - prefer a make target for the command so the workflow is discoverable and   \
  repeatable\n\
  - tests should cover the cache/write/read path even if the browser step is   \
  manual\n\
  - rerun `make pipeline` and commit the resulting `data/` diffs if the     \
extraction/parsing behavior changes\n\
\n\
This issue is complete when a developer can recover one of the known 403/429 \
URLs by driving the browser manually and the pipeline can then snapshot it \
from the local cache.",
